WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.599
Good morning Hank. It's Tuesday.

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.240
So at the end of every year I write myself two letters:

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.160
one to the me from the past who just survived the year

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.900
and one to the me from the future who's going to be living in next year.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.800
And I look at the successes and failures and joys and heartbreaks

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.280
of the last year and then make goals for next year.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.400
This is a thing that was suggested by my therapist and

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.600
initially I responded to it with the same suspicion I reserve for all therapy things.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.859
But actually I found it incredibly helpful because, first, it offers an opportunity for reflection

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:28.800
and I feel like there aren't enough of those in our culture right now.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.280
It also gives you an opportunity to think about what from 2017 you want to

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.660
bring into 2018 and what you want to leave behind.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:39.920
So today I thought I would write kind of an open public version of that letter to myself

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:44.180
in the hopes of improving accountability, called 'Leave it in 2017!'

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.500
All right, listen up future me.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:50.440
There's a few things that current me is going to recommend you leave behind in 2017, beginning with

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:55.280
'The Feed'. You know what I'm talking about; the fact that you spend hours every day passively scrolling through

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:58.580
information on the internet that does not leave you better informed or happier.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:01.640
I'm not saying that the social Internet is inherently good or bad.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.320
I'm saying that right now, it's bad for you.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.600
So you're gonna LEAVE IT IN 2017!

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.880
At least for a few months, until your resolve begins to crack because

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:13.480
the social media algorithms have so effectively hacked your dopamine receptors.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:16.800
Slight tangent, but the reason your social media feeds aren't making you happier

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:19.520
or better informed is that they're not trying to.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.280
The algorithms are designed to keep you scrolling, to maximize the amount of time you spend on the site,

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:29.540
and ergo, the number of ad impressions you provide, and the algorithms are very good at doing that.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:32.380
But on the other hand, John, you have a habit of believing that something,

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:33.840
say, the social Internet for instance,

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.020
is going to be the solution to all of humanity's problems, and then when it fails to deliver

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:41.320
you have a habit of over-correcting, and announcing that, for instance,

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.520
the social Internet is nothing but a pile of burnt garbage.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.380
But both those extremes are equally invalid,

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:51.360
so we're leaving those wild swings behind in 2017 as well, and instead embracing something called nuance.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.020
Speaking of wild swings: Let's talk about sports. So, sports are great.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:02:00.160
But with AFC Wimbledon currently in the league one relegation zone and Liverpool tossing away 3-nil leads

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:02.380
like Luke Skywalker chucking lightsabers,

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.000
too much of your mental well-being depends upon the sporting endeavors of

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.760
eleven mostly 20-something strangers who live 5,000 miles away from you.

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:13.520
I'm not saying don't care. I'm saying don't shout

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.000
'Everything is ruined all your dreams will die!' at the television, in front of your children.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.240
All right four more things you're leaving in 2017:

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.500
One: Prioritizing your career over your sanity. That's dumb. Stop doing that.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:29.280
Secondly: Drinking more than two La Croix per day. I mean La Croix is great, but show some restraint.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.580
Three: Being snarky in the comments when you feel the urge to provoke,

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:36.100
or to respond to trolling, or to meet hostility with hostility.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:37.840
Just stop!

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.140
And lastly, somewhat related:

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:42.740
Let's leave in 2017 the notion that the avatar is the person

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:46.660
and instead try to remember that everyone we encounter online and off

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.180
is fighting battles that we don't know about.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.400
Now, there's also lots I want to keep from 2017:

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:53.320
the life-changing magic of regular exercise,

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:56.780
the value of real-life events to celebrate usually virtual communities,

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:59.580
and the reminders of why I love not only writing,

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:03.180
but also sharing my writing--even if it's a smidge terrifying.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:07.880
I'd love to know in comments what you're gonna hold on to from 2017, and also what you want to leave behind.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:11.080
Hank, Happy New Year. I will see you on Friday

